{"id":"med-coast-light","familyId":"med-coast","size":"light","name":"Mediterranean Coast (Light)","cuisine":"Mediterranean","style":"balanced","carbStyle":"whole","covers":[[1400,1600],[1600,1800]],"summary":"A week of the way people eat along the Mediterranean coast: beans and lentils most days, olive oil, plenty of vegetables, oily fish twice plus shrimp, and a little feta and yogurt. Greek, Southern Italian and Spanish dishes built on potatoes, legumes, barley and oats.","howItWorks":"**Cook once, eat twice.** Monday's lemon chicken, Wednesday's lentil soup, Friday's chickpea stew and Saturday's saffron barley each make two portions, so the next day's lunch is done. Saturday's tortilla also gives you a cold-slice breakfast on Sunday.\n\n**Quick lunches.** A meze plate on Monday, a village salad on Wednesday and a Sicilian fava, orange and fennel salad on Friday come together in minutes.\n\n**Fish three times.** Sardines on Tuesday, salmon on Thursday and shrimp on Sunday never land back to back, and are always cooked fresh. Sardines and salmon bring **omega-3 and vitamin D**, and the sardines' soft bones add calcium.\n\n**Beans, eggs and greens.** Legumes show up most days and eggs every day, for **choline**. Kale, yogurt and feta cover calcium, and lentils, chickpeas and fava beans cover iron. Walnuts and a spoonful of chia in the oats balance the omega-6 from olive oil.\n\n**Sunday prep.** Blitz a batch of hummus (it feeds Monday's meze plate and Thursday's snack) and mix the first jar of oats, then repeat on Tuesday and Thursday nights. Save your energy for **Saturday**, when the tortilla and saffron barley take more time.","signature":["lemon-chicken-potatoes","sardines-white-beans-kale","fakes-lentil-soup","shrimp-saganaki-potatoes"],"regions":["Greece","Southern Italy","Spain"],"days":[{"day":1,"meals":[{"recipe":"yogurt-oats-fig-almond","slot":"breakfast","g":[30,140,30,10,8,5],"heroes":[{"key":"manganese","pct":0.94},{"key":"phosphorus","pct":0.65}]},{"r